
Filesystem type on dedicated ZFS root pool slice isn't zfs type:
================================================================
It is possible that previous installations left over old partition
signatures or fragments, which prevent a successfull installation.
None multiboot installations typically show the following VTOC layout:

Part      Tag    Flag     First Sector          Size          Last Sector
  0     system    wm               256       256.00MB           524543    
  1        usr    wm            524544       476.68GB           1000198798    
  2 unassigned    wm                 0            0                0    
  3 unassigned    wm                 0            0                0    
  4 unassigned    wm                 0            0                0    
  5 unassigned    wm                 0            0                0    
  6 unassigned    wm                 0            0                0    
  8   reserved    wm        1000198799         8.00MB           1000215182    

During implementation of multiboot setups, it has been discoverd that it
is possible to create ZFS filesystems on a PCFS filesystem type, but later
the installboot command fails on the rpool slice, with error message:

Booting pcfs from EFI labeled disks requires the boot partition.

If you should see this message, please re-check filesystem types of the
first parition slice:

# /usr/sbin/fstyp /dev/rdsk/<your disk>s0
  it should respond with: unknown_fstyp (no_matches)

Then check the partition slice on which you expect the ZFS root filesystem:

# /usr/sbin/fstyp /dev/rdsk/<your disk>s1
  it should respond with: zfs

If one or both of these slices respond different, first destroy the ZFS
rpool, which might already exist:
# zpool list
NAME    SIZE  ALLOC   FREE  CKPOINT  EXPANDSZ   FRAG    CAP  DEDUP    HEALTH...
rpool   476G   540K   476G        -         -     0%     0%  1.00x    ONLINE  -

# zpool destroy rpool

Next step is to clean the signatures of the slice(s) using dd:
# dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/rdsk/<your disk>s0 bs=1M count=16
# dd if=/dev/zero of=/dev/rdsk/<your disk>s1 bs=1M count=16

Last step is to revert the partition table using the format program:
# format
or you use a combination of prtvtoc / fmthard:
# prtvtoc -h /dev/rdsk/<disk>s0 | tail -1 | fmthard -s - /dev/rdsk/<disk>s0

response should be:
fmthard: New volume table of contents now in place.

After all these steps are done, restart the installer by typing exit in this
SHELL and start all over by choosing "1" in the top-level menu of the installer
and continue by choosing your installation disk again.
Your installation should be working from now on.

=================
Multiboot Setups:
=================

SPARC:
======
On SPARC systems a multiboot setup is much more simple than on x86 based systems,
because the boot-manager is already build in. The OBP (Open Boot Prom) provides
the possibility to boot from arbitrary disks. If you are short on disks you can
even boot from one of the standard VTOC slices: 0-7 directly from OBP.
Here are some examples and hints how to boot/install on SPARC systems:

tbn...

x86_64 BIOS/MBR:
================

currently not supported.

x86_64 EFI/UEFI/GPT:
====================
For EFI/UEFI or GPT systems it may be necessary to disable "Secure Boot", in
your system BIOS, in order to be able to boot from USB/DVD media.

Possible Multiboot Scenarios:
=============================
1. illumos + Windows OR illumos + Windows + Linux
-------------------------------------------------
If you plan a multiboot setup, involving also a Windows installation on a
UEFI/GPT system, things are getting more complicated. At present this
installer detects your Windows installation and checks it's size. If there is
not enough space available for a illumos installation, it offers you the
possibility to resize the disk for a new windows installation. At present it's
not possible to shrink the existing Windows installation. Therefore only a new
installation of Windows on predefined partition tables ensures a stable
illumos installation. The predefined partitions for Windows, offered by this
installer define the following standard Windows sizes:

  1. for EFI as the system partition 100MB.
  2. a Windows reserved partition 16MB.
  3. NTFS - the size you'll choose while installing.
Note: the 100MB system parition will be shared by all operating systems.

During installation the installer asks for:
Do you want to setup a Multiboot Environment (default=N/y?):
If you answer "y" the following menu appears:

	1 OpenIndiana + Linux
	2 OpenIndiana + Windows
	3 OpenIndiana + Windows + Linux
	q  back to disk selection

	Your choice: 

If you choose option 2 or 3 the installer asks for the size of your
Windows partition and creates above partition layout with the size
you want to spent for Windows. Immediatley after that it reboots
your system for a new Windws installation, indicated by:

Rebooting for Windows Install...

At the Windows installation screen make sure to select thr NTFS
partition - usually partition 3 and press the format button
before installling Windows, leave all other partitions untouched.
After your new Windows installation has been done, restart this
installer and choose the same disk as before, now you'll not be
asked to enable a Multboot Environment. You should see the
following messages instead:

Welcome back to OpenIndiana installation, the selected Windows
layout uses: xxxGB

Next step will be to set the size for the illumos installation, and
or the Linux installation, devide the rest of the disk space between
them as you see fit.

If you have choosen a config which includes Linux, you see the
following mesage right at the end of illumos install:

NOTES:
illumos starts to count partitions starting with 0
Linux starts to count partitions starting with 1
If you decide NOT to use a Linux installation with or without Windows,
you have to use some UEFI firmware/application to boot illumos, which
can usually be found in your system/UEFI frimware settings.

2. illumos + Linux
------------------
If you have choosen option 1 from above menu, you have to decide how much
disk space you want to spent for illumos, the remainig space will be used
for Linux. This installer will create a script for you which can be used
to add the illumos installation into the grub configuration of your
Linux installation, shown by the following message:

You have to run: /boot/efi/EFI/OpenIndiana/add_grub,
after the Linux installation has been done, to activate
OpenIndiana in your grub configuration!

In the Linux installer you will see one partition with the size you have
specified for Linux. Select this partition in the Linux installer, and
press the "-" button, now this space should appear as free-space.
Divide this free space into at least 2 partitions - one for swap and
one ext4 for / by pressing the "+" sign.
Make sure you DO NOT TOUCH parition 9 as it marks the start of the
entire disk and if modified, you'll have to start all over again.

Hardware Notes:
===============
1.) ACER Aspire3 laptops (A315-54K):
The disk-controller "SATA Mode" in the "Information" tab of the system BIOS
is set to "Optane without RAID" by default. Therefore illumos will not see
any disk drives. Switch to the next tab "Main" and press <Crtl>+S to hideout
the "SATA Mode" menu, showing above setting, select this sub-menu and press
<Return>, choose <AHCI> mode. Exit system BIOS saving your change, and
re-enter BIOS to verify the "Information" tab shows AHCI mode now, before
trying to install illumos/OpenIndiana.
Note: this will definitively render your Windows installation unbootable!
To fix this, you can have a look at Ubuntu's Web-pages, if you want to keep
your Windows installation as is. They have excellent help pages to work
around that problem. I found it much more easier to just re-install Windows
in AHCI mode for my tests.
